Ladybird indexes transform animations by their target layout node,
keeping pseudo-element effects separate from effects on their hosts.
This matches the target-box distinction in Blink, WebKit, and Gecko.

Blink keeps the generated PseudoElement as effect_target_ through
KeyframeEffect::UpdateEffectTarget(), and uses that target for its
compositor eligibility checks and layout-object lookup. WebKit uses
KeyframeEffect::targetStyleable() to retain the pseudo identifier;
KeyframeEffect::renderer() then delegates to Styleable::renderer().

Gecko selects the generated frame in KeyframeEffect::GetPrimaryFrame()
from mTarget.mPseudoRequest. Its before and after cases call
nsLayoutUtils::GetBeforeFrame() and nsLayoutUtils::GetAfterFrame(),
keeping their animation targets distinct from the originating element.
